<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Lets the test host find a system-installed libvips on macOS. Shared by every suite that loads NetVips:
  the integration suite directly, and the three E2E suites because they host (or spawn) the real Api.

  The Infrastructure project references only the linux-musl natives — that is what the Alpine image ships, and
  until the inbox page operations (issue #487) nothing in the test suite touched NetVips, so nobody noticed
  that NEITHER test environment has a libvips: not a developer's Mac, and not the glibc CI runner. The first
  TIFF test failed on both with DllNotFoundException.

  The fix is a system package rather than three more NetVips.Native.* NuGet packages — those would extend the
  LGPL exception in build/licenses/ignored-packages.json to platforms the product does not ship, for a
  test-only need. So: `brew install vips` locally, and the libvips runtime package on the runner (see
  CLAUDE.md's Commands and the install step in ci.yml).

  Linux then needs nothing further: apt installs into the default loader path. Homebrew on Apple Silicon does
  not — /opt/homebrew/lib is not searched by dyld — hence the variable below. It must be set before the test
  host starts (dyld reads it at launch), which is exactly what a .runsettings does and what an in-process
  DllImportResolver cannot: NetVips installs its own resolver in a module initializer, and a second one throws
  "A resolver is already set for the assembly".

  DYLD_* is ignored on Linux and Windows, so this is a no-op off macOS. The path list covers Apple Silicon and
  Intel Homebrew; FALLBACK (not DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH) so it is consulted only after the normal search, and cannot
  shadow a library the runtime resolves correctly on its own.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <EnvironmentVariables>
      <DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH>/opt/homebrew/lib:/usr/local/lib:/usr/lib</DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH>
    </EnvironmentVariables>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
